I see you have some good practical reasons to use ginv instead of
lm.fit. But my guess would be that, when lm.fit returns NA, it is for
good, and I would rather say that the numbers you get out from ginv
%*% are rubbish in those cases.
The ideal solution would be to either propagate the NAs in the results
when this happens, or to report a meaningful error message. But I do
understand that this can be quite cumbersome to get right (and I'm not
doing to do it...), so a try-catch solution is probably the best :-)

btw, great work guys!
bests,
fabio.

2011/5/11 Matthieu Stigler <matthieu...@gmail.com>:

> Christoph
>
> Thanks for checking and solving so fast, great!
>
> On the ginv issue: I use somewhere a try() and test, which might be a
> solution as: by default use lm.fit() (or solve in my case), and if problem
> use ginv(), see:
>  VVinv<-try(solve(VV), silent=TRUE)
>      if(inherits(VVinv, "try-error")) VVinv<-ginv(VV)
>
> On the star -> linear issue: indeed, would be nice if you could then return
> a ar (see function linear() ) when 1 regime only!
>
> Thanks!!
>
> Mat
